{"abstract":"Background: A new primary cancer is a serious late effect of a pre-existing cancer diagnosis, and can be attributed to hereditary cancer syndromes, immune or hormonal factors, cancer treatment, or modifiable lifestyle or environmental factors. We investigated the absolute and relative incidence of second primary cancers in a large cohort of Danish cancer survivors. Furthermore, we examined the association between alcohol-related, smoking-related, virus-related, and hormone-related first and second primary cancers. Methods: In this retrospective cohort study, we identified a cohort of Danish adults (aged ≥40 years) diagnosed with cancer from Jan 1, 1997, to Dec 31, 2014 and alive 1 year after diagnosis. Follow-up was from date of first cancer diagnosis and lasted up to 24 years, ending on Dec 31, 2020. Cohort identification and information on second primary cancers was obtained from the Danish Cancer Registry, and comorbidity and sociodemographic information was obtained from Danish population-based registries. Overall, and for 27 cancer types, cumulative incidence functions and Cox proportional hazard regression models were used to estimate the incidence of second primary cancer and death, and hazard ratios (HRs) and 95% CIs of second primary cancer adjusted for sex, age and year of diagnosis, cohabitation status, income, and comorbidity. Findings: 457 334 Danish adults were included in our study (230 150 [50·3%] male individuals and 227 184 [49·7%] female individuals; median age at diagnosis 68·3 years, IQR 59·7–76·6; median follow-up 3·6 years, IQR 0·6–9·3). The cumulative incidence of second primary cancer increased over time from 6·3% (95% CI 6·2–6·4) 5 years after diagnosis to 10·5% (10·4–10·6) 10 years after diagnosis and to 13·5% (13·4–13·7) 15 years after diagnosis. The highest cumulative incidence of second primary cancer 10 years after diagnosis was observed in survivors of cancers in the larynx (21·8%, 20·5–23·1), oropharynx and oral cavity (19·5%, 18·7–20·3), and bladder and urinary tract (18·5%, 18·0–19·0). Survivors of cancers related to alcohol (HR 1·09, 95% CI 1·06–1·13), smoking (1·73, 1·68–1·78), diet high in red or processed meat (1·32, 1·24–1·39), or virus (1·23, 1·13–1·35) were at increased risk of developing a second cancer with the same aetiology, whereas having had a hormone-related first cancer was associated with lower risk of a second hormone-related cancer (0·77, 0·73–0·81). Interpretation: Our results could help optimise prevention efforts targeting modifiable risk factors to reduce risk of developing a second primary cancer. Funding: Nordic Cancer Union and The Health Foundation (Helsefonden).","author_count":9,"contributors":["Kjaer, Trille Kristina","Andersen, Elisabeth Anne Wreford","Ursin, Giske","Larsen, Signe Benzon","Bidstrup, Pernille Envold","Winther, Jeanette Falck","Borre, Michael","Johansen, Christoffer","Dalton, Susanne Oksbjerg"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-721897"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Cancer Registry of Norway","University of Oslo","University of Southern California"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cumulative incidence of second primary cancers in a large nationwide cohort of danish cancer survivors a population based retrospective cohort study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-721897","identifier":[{"id":"85178573337","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38048803","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-cd566a59-7d49-49e5-83ac-4a00b03f16c1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EB9DD5CAF4EC71233AD08876F1A6FDA6"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-CAN","l3nn":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","namevar":["Danish Cancer Society"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-CAN","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"cancer registry of norway","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Cancer Registry of Norway","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Psychology, Department of Psychology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"9","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Næstved, Slagelse and Ringsted"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Trille Kristina","key":"kjaerpau","last":"Kjaer","name":"Kjaer, Trille Kristina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Elisabeth Anne Wreford","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Elisabeth Anne Wreford","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3 4","first":"Giske","key":"ursinpau","last":"Ursin","name":"Ursin, Giske","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","first":"Signe Benzon","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Signe Benzon","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 6","first":"Pernille Envold","key":"bidstruppau","last":"Bidstrup","locid":"7cef3894-8d41-4b9b-acec-95b1d1b3f890","name":"Bidstrup, Pernille Envold","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 7","first":"Jeanette Falck","key":"wintherpau","last":"Winther","name":"Winther, Jeanette Falck","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7 8","first":"Michael","key":"borrepau","last":"Borre","name":"Borre, Michael","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Christoffer","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","name":"Johansen, Christoffer","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 6 9","first":"Susanne Oksbjerg","key":"daltonpau","last":"Dalton","locid":"84d10468-fe5d-4cc7-952f-1d89d961390f","name":"Dalton, Susanne Oksbjerg","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38048803"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/s1470-2045(23)00538-7","issn":["14702045","14745488"],"issn_org":["14702045"],"issue":"1","journal":"The Lancet Oncology","pages":"126-136","status":"p","title":"The Lancet Oncology","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85178573337"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-cd566a59-7d49-49e5-83ac-4a00b03f16c1"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cumulative incidence of second primary cancers in a large nationwide cohort of Danish cancer survivors : a population-based retrospective cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-721897","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This study presents an interdisciplinary approach to analyze different transition pathways towards the sustainable development of a low-carbon society, focusing on Norway as a case. The study bridges a socio-technical perspective on sustainability transitions with techno-economic energy systems and regional-economic modelling analyses. Incorporating a socio-technical perspective in the scenario design allows us to envision pathways considering causal processes of technological and socio-institutional change, and potential transition bottlenecks. The resulting scenarios are used in the techno-economic energy system analysis to show cost-optimal energy system configurations, including varying levels of new renewable capacity needed, new conversion technologies, and fuel substitutions across all sectors leading to different decarbonization pathways for the Norwegian energy system by 2050. The regional-economy analysis addresses the impacts of these pathways on general economic growth and labor. The results show that higher levels of decarbonization are possible for Norway; however, potential bottlenecks can slow down the transition, while trade-offs in economic growth and development must be balanced out with decarbonization ambitions.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Chang, Miguel","Espegren, Kari","Haaskjold, Kristina","Rosenberg, Eva","Damman, Sigrid","Mäkitie, Tuukka","Pisciella, Paolo","Andersen, Allan Dahl","Skjølsvold, Tomas Moe"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-755021"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Institute for Energy Technology","Norwegian University of Science and Technology","SINTEF","University of Oslo"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"transition pathways for a low carbon norway bridging socio technical and energy system analyses","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-58897-6_9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-755021","identifier":[{"id":"85198457886","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-f40250a9-1ece-45b4-a47f-36a57175d5f0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8235B35E3321FD071747E3ECD501F2B3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/401796818/Transition_Pathways_for_a_Low_Carbon_Norway_Bridging_Socio_technical_and_Energy_System_Analyses.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-58897-6_9","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"no","key":"institute for energy technology","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Institute for Energy Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"sintef","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SINTEF","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"no","key":"norwegian university of science and technology","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Norwegian University of Science and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Food and Resource Economics, Section for Production, Markets and Policy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Miguel","key":"changpau","last":"Chang","name":"Chang, Miguel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kari","key":"espegrenpau","last":"Espegren","name":"Espegren, Kari","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kristina","key":"haaskjoldpau","last":"Haaskjold","name":"Haaskjold, Kristina","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eva","key":"rosenbergpau","last":"Rosenberg","name":"Rosenberg, Eva","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Sigrid","key":"dammanpau","last":"Damman","name":"Damman, Sigrid","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Tuukka","key":"mäkitiepau","last":"Mäkitie","name":"Mäkitie, Tuukka","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Paolo","key":"pisciellapau","last":"Pisciella","name":"Pisciella, Paolo","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 5","first":"Allan Dahl","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a98959b6-c7d5-41d0-884b-2296f5f60c4f","name":"Andersen, Allan Dahl","orcid":"0000-0002-2703-293X","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Tomas Moe","key":"skjølsvoldpau","last":"Skjølsvold","name":"Skjølsvold, Tomas Moe","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Maryse","key":"labrietpedh","last":"Labriet","name":"Labriet, Maryse","rank":10,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kari","key":"espegrenpedh","last":"Espegren","name":"Espegren, Kari","rank":11,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"George","key":"giannakidispedh","last":"Giannakidis","name":"Giannakidis, George","rank":12,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Brian","key":"ógallachóirpedh","last":"Ó Gallachóir","name":"Ó Gallachóir, Brian","rank":13,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-58897-6_9","eisbn":["9783031588976"],"isbn":["9783031588969","9783031588990"],"issn":["21951284"],"pages":"197-222","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Energy","status":"p","subtitle":"Key Insights from Energy System Modelling","title":"Aligning the Energy Transition with the Sustainable Development Goals"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85198457886"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f40250a9-1ece-45b4-a47f-36a57175d5f0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Transition Pathways for a Low-Carbon Norway : Bridging Socio-technical and Energy System Analyses","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-755021","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH) is a common but frequently unrecognized complication of obesity and type 2 diabetes. The association between these conditions is multifaceted and involves complex interactions between metabolic, inflammatory, and genetic factors. Here we assess the underlying structural and molecular processes focusing on the immunological phase of MASH in the nonobese inflammation and fibrosis (NIF) mouse model and compare it to the human disease as well as other murine models. Histopathology together with synchrotron-radiation-based x-ray micro-computed tomography (SRµCT) was used to investigate structural changes within the hepatic sinusoids network in the NIF mouse in comparison to patients with different severities of MASH. A time-course, bulk RNA-sequencing analysis of liver tissue from NIF mice was performed to identify the dynamics of key processes associated with the pathogenesis. Transcriptomics profiling of the NIF mouse revealed a gradual transition from an initially reactive inflammatory response to a regenerative, pro-fibrotic inflammatory response suggesting new avenues for treatment strategies that focus on immunological targets. Despite the lack of metabolic stress induced liver phenotype, a large similarity between the NIF mouse and the immunological phase of human MASH was detected. The translational value was further supported by the comparative analyses with MASH patients and additional animal models. Finally, the impact of diets known to induce metabolic stress, was explored in the NIF mouse. An obesogenic diet was found to induce key physiological, metabolic, and histologic changes akin to those observed in human MASH.","author_count":17,"contributors":["Schmidt-Christensen, Anja","Eriksson, Gustaw","Laprade, William Michael","Pirzamanbein, Behnaz","Hörnberg, Maria","Linde, Kajsa","Nilsson, Julia","Skarsfeldt, Mark","Leeming, Diana J.","Mokso, Rajmund","Verezhak, Mariana","Dahl, Anders Bjorho