{"abstract":"A technique to image α-synuclein (αSYN) fibrils in vivo is an unmet scientific and clinical need that would represent a transformative tool in the understanding, diagnosis, and treatment of various neurodegenerative diseases. Several classes of compounds have shown promising results as potential PET tracers, but no candidate has yet exhibited the affinity and selectivity required to reach clinical application. We hypothesized that the application of the rational drug design technique of molecular hybridization to two promising lead scaffolds could enhance the binding to αSYN up to the fulfillment of those requirements. By combining the structures of SIL and MODAG tracers, we developed a library of diarylpyrazoles (DAPs). In vitro evaluation through competition assays against [3H]SIL26 and [3H]MODAG−001 showed the novel hybrid scaffold to have preferential binding affinity for amyloid β (Aβ) over αSYN fibrils. A ring-opening modification on the phenothiazine building block to produce analogs with increased three-dimensional flexibility did not result in an improved αSYN binding but a complete loss of competition, as well as a significant reduction in Aβ affinity. The combination of the phenothiazine and the 3,5-diphenylpyrazole scaffolds into DAP hybrids did not generate an enhanced αSYN PET tracer lead compound. Instead, these efforts identified a scaffold for promising Aβ ligands that may be relevant to the treatment and monitoring of Alzheimer’s disease (AD).","author_count":10,"contributors":["Di Nanni, Adriana","Saw, Ran Sing","Bowden, Gregory D.","Bidesi, Natasha Shalina Rajani","Bjerregaard-Andersen, Kaare","Korat, Špela","Herth, Matthias M.","Pichler, Bernd J.","Herfert, Kristina","Maurer, Andreas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-679516"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Amsterdam UMC","University of Tübingen","Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam"],"keyword":["DLB","MSA","PD","PET tracer","Positron Emission Tomography","α Synuclein"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-LUND"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-LUND","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the structural combination of sil and modag scaffolds fails to enhance binding to α synuclein but reveals promising affinity to amyloid β","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules28104001","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-679516","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10224536","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85160373000","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37241742","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-f7fd92d7-e591-4962-ac2a-9571050765ef"],"keyword":["3,5-diphenylpyrazole","DLB","MSA","PD","PET tracer","phenothiazine","positron emission tomography","α-synuclein"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B8C473545B190B29DDA4D36E5C16AE01"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules28104001","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/357279300/2.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"university of tübingen","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Tübingen","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology, Medicinal Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"lundbeck","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-LUND","l3nl":"Lundbeck Pharma","l3nn":"Lundbeck","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lundbeck","namevar":["Lundbeck"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-LUND","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"nl","key":"vrije universiteit amsterdam","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"nl","key":"amsterdam umc","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Amsterdam UMC","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Adriana","key":"dinannipau","last":"Di Nanni","name":"Di Nanni, Adriana","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ran Sing","key":"sawpau","last":"Saw","name":"Saw, Ran Sing","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Gregory D.","key":"bowdenpau","last":"Bowden","name":"Bowden, Gregory D.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Natasha S.R.","key":"bidesipau","last":"Bidesi","locid":"ce4cb424-7de1-453b-bc0d-cd469bd31a5e","name":"Bidesi, Natasha Shalina Rajani","name_org":"Bidesi, Natasha S.R.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Kaare","key":"bjerregaardandersenpau","last":"Bjerregaard-Andersen","name":"Bjerregaard-Andersen, Kaare","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","first":"Špela","key":"koratpau","last":"Korat","name":"Korat, Špela","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Matthias M.","key":"herthpau","last":"Herth","locid":"001339a3-ac7c-4378-bfd6-c13d689a5f6f","name":"Herth, Matthias M.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bernd J.","key":"pichlerpau","last":"Pichler","name":"Pichler, Bernd J.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kristina","key":"herfertpau","last":"Herfert","name":"Herfert, Kristina","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Andreas","key":"maurerpau","last":"Maurer","name":"Maurer, Andreas","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37241742"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/molecules28104001","issn":["14203049","14315157"],"issn_org":["14203049"],"issue":"10","journal":"Molecules","paperid":"4001","status":"p","title":"Molecules","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160373000"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Lundbeck Pharma","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f7fd92d7-e591-4962-ac2a-9571050765ef"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Structural Combination of SIL and MODAG Scaffolds Fails to Enhance Binding to α-Synuclein but Reveals Promising Affinity to Amyloid β","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-679516","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Objective: The AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) gets activated in response to energetic stress such as contractions and plays a vital role in regulating various metabolic processes such as insulin-independent glucose uptake in skeletal muscle. The main upstream kinase that activates AMPK through phosphorylation of α-AMPK Thr172 in skeletal muscle is LKB1, however some studies have suggested that Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase kinase 2 (CaMKK2) acts as an alternative kinase to activate AMPK. We aimed to establish whether CaMKK2 is involved in activation of AMPK and promotion of glucose uptake following contractions in skeletal muscle. Methods: A recently developed CaMKK2 inhibitor (SGC-CAMKK2-1) alongside a structurally related but inactive compound (SGC-CAMKK2-1N), as well as CaMKK2 knock-out (KO) mice were used. In vitro kinase inhibition selectivity and efficacy assays, as well as cellular inhibition efficacy analyses of CaMKK inhibitors (STO-609 and SGC-CAMKK2-1) were performed. Phosphorylation and activity of AMPK following contractions (ex vivo) in mouse skeletal muscles treated with/without CaMKK inhibitors or isolated from wild-type (WT)/CaMKK2 KO mice were assessed. Camkk2 mRNA in mouse tissues was measured by qPCR. CaMKK2 protein expression was assessed by immunoblotting with or without prior enrichment of calmodulin-binding proteins from skeletal muscle extracts, as well as by mass spectrometry-based proteomics of mouse skeletal muscle and C2C12 myotubes. Results: STO-609 and SGC-CAMKK2-1 were equally potent and effective in inhibiting CaMKK2 in cell-free and cell-based assays, but SGC-CAMKK2-1 was much more selective. Contraction-stimulated phosphorylation and activation of AMPK were not affected with CaMKK inhibitors or in CaMKK2 null muscles. Contraction-stimulated glucose uptake was comparable between WT and CaMKK2 KO muscle. Both CaMKK inhibitors (STO-609 and SGC-CAMKK2-1) and the inactive compound (SGC-CAMKK2-1N) significantly inhibited contraction-stimulated glucose uptake. SGC-CAMKK2-1 also inhibited glucose uptake induced by a pharmacological AMPK activator or insulin. Relatively low levels of Camkk2 mRNA were detected in mouse skeletal muscle, but neither CaMKK2 protein nor its derived peptides were detectable in mouse skeletal muscle tissue. Conclusions: We demonstrate that pharmacological inhibition or genetic loss of CaMKK2 does not affect contraction-stimulated AMPK phosphorylation and activation, as well as glucose uptake in skeletal muscle. Previously observed inhibitory effect of STO-609 on AMPK activity and glucose uptake is likely due to off-target effects. CaMKK2 protein is either absent from adult murine skeletal muscle or below the detection limit of currently available methods.","author_count":21,"contributors":["Negoita, Florentina","Addinsall, Alex B.","Hellberg, Kristina","Bringas, Conchita Fraguas","Hafen, Paul S.","Sermersheim, Tyler J.","Andersen, Marianne Agerholm","Lewis, Christopher T.A.","Ahwazi, Danial","Ling, Naomi X.Y.","Larsen, Jeppe Kjærgaard","Deshmukh, Atul S.","Hossain, Mohammad A.","Oakhill, Jonathan S.","Ochala, Julien","Brault, Jeffrey J.","Sankar, Uma","Drewry, David H.","Scott, John W.","Witczak, Carol A.","Sakamoto, Kei"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-684395"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Florey Institute of Neuroscience and Mental Health","Indiana University Bloomington","Indiana University-Purdue University Columbus","Monash University","St Vincent's Institute of Medical Research","University of Melbourne","University of North Carolina","University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill"],"keyword":["AMP activated protein kinase","Glucose uptake","STO 609"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"camkk000000000002 is not involved in contraction stimulated ampk activation and glucose uptake in skeletal muscle","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molmet.2023.101761","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-684395","identifier":[{"id":"85164661974","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37380024","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10362367","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-b34f4a5a-8314-4d15-81b2-2ee7424b8440"],"keyword":["AMP-activated protein kinase","Ca/calmodulin dependent protein kinase kinase 2","Glucose uptake","SGC-CAMKK2-1","STO-609"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-98E7B36D035ECD04703AE6D1FBF425A8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molmet.2023.101761","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/360603361/1_s2.0_S2212877823000959.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Sakamoto Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"novo nordisk","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-NOVO","l3nl":"Novo Nordisk","l3nn":"Novo Nordisk","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Novo Nordisk","namevar":["Novo Nordisk"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-NOVO","used":1},{"affno":"3","countr