,"name_org":"Benn, Christine S.","orcid":"0000-0001-6102-3810","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Scott J.","key":"tebbuttpau","last":"Tebbutt","name":"Tebbutt, Scott J.","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 15","corresponding":null,"first":"Tobias R.","key":"kollmannpau","last":"Kollmann","name":"Kollmann, Tobias R.","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 15","corresponding":null,"first":"Nelly","key":"amenyogbepau","last":"Amenyogbe","name":"Amenyogbe, Nelly","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32376769"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1126/scitranslmed.aax4517","issn":["19466234","19466242"],"issn_org":["19466234"],"issue":"542","journal":"Science Translational Medicine","paperid":"eaax4517","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science","status":"p","title":"Science Translational Medicine","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85084387993"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-90347627-b998-4404-b9e0-3d552206e81a"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"BCG vaccination-induced emergency granulopoiesis provides rapid protection from neonatal sepsis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-614540","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Labor market participation has a positive impact on social inclusion and is linked to financial security. This study identifies barriers and willingness to accept re-employment among unemployed seniors that could highlight opportunities for societal action. From the first wave of SeniorWorkingLife in 2018 combined with the Danish version of the International Standard Classification of Occupations register (ISCO), +50-year-old unemployed senior workers (n = 1682) were stratified into mainly seated work (ISCO 1-4) and mainly physical work (ISCO 5-9), respectively, in their latest employment. We used SurveyFreq and SurveyLogistics of SAS combined with model-assisted weights based on national registers to estimate representative frequencies and odds ratios (OR) for barriers and willingness to accept re-employment. Higher age was perceived as a general barrier for re-employment in both groups. Health was a more pronounced barrier for seniors with mainly physical work compared to seniors with mainly seated work (OR 2.35; CI95 1.31-4.21). Overall, seniors showed a large degree of flexibility and willingness to re-enter the labor market. Different barriers and willingness to accept re-employment exist among currently unemployed seniors. These results highlight the need for different approaches across occupational groups to help unemployed seniors back into the labor market.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Thomassen, Kristina","Sundstrup, Emil","Skovlund, Sebastian Venge","Andersen, Lars Louis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-780932"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Seniorer"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"barriers and willingness to accept re employment among unemployed senior workers the seniorworkinglife study","src":["nfa"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17155358","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-780932","identifier":[{"id":"85088679100","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32722360","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["nfa-75c34244-239e-4a2c-8b84-d3d4c586be9d"],"keyword":["Seniorer"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-32E6F45A9CE5F4C4BC84992D608CB910"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17155358","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"nfa national research centre for the working environment","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-WORK","l3nn":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","namevar":["The National Research Centre for the Working Environment","The National Research Centre for the Working Environment, Directors, Musculoskeletal Disorders and Physical Workload"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-WORK","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristina","key":"thomassenpau","last":"Thomassen","name":"Thomassen, Kristina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Emil","key":"sundstruppau","last":"Sundstrup","locid":"688eab9a-3ad9-48b8-aad2-397c0a8ee105","name":"Sundstrup, Emil","orcid":"0000-0001-7408-222X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian V","key":"skovlundpau","last":"Skovlund","locid":"7ed308b6-7f4e-45d9-b04e-9d690a0cbfa2","name":"Skovlund, Sebastian Venge","name_org":"Skovlund, Sebastian V","orcid":"0000-0002-9820-2895","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars L","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"7918e862-7513-454a-874b-3fdb31af5e48","name":"Andersen, Lars Louis","name_org":"Andersen, Lars L","orcid":"0000-0003-2777-8085","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32722360"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/ijerph17155358","issn":["16604601","16617827"],"issn_org":["16617827"],"issue":"15","journal":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","paperid":"5358","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","volume":"17"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088679100"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","Governmental Institutions"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["nfa-75c34244-239e-4a2c-8b84-d3d4c586be9d"],"src":["NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment"],"title":"Barriers and willingness to accept re-employment among unemployed senior workers: The SeniorWorkingLife Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-780932","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Bladder dysfunction may occur in acute stroke patients and as a consequence the risk of urinary retention, incontinence and urinary tract infections increases. The literature is sparse regarding occurrence of bladder dysfunction in acute stroke and if catheterization should be performed to prevent bladder overdistension in acute stroke thrombolysis. The aim of this study was to investigate the safety of implementing a new selective and “watchful waiting strategy” for intermittent catheterization during acute stroke thrombolysis. From November 2018 to June 2019, we conducted a prospective cohort study before and after implementing (in March 2019) a selective and watchful waiting strategy for intermittent catheterization. Data were collected on nurse-administered registration forms for ischemic stroke patients treated with thrombolysis. In both periods urogenital complications were registered. We found no significant differences between period 1 (before) and 2 (after) in the occurrence of urinary tract infections and/or macroscopic urogenital bleeding episodes. However, for developing blood in the 24hour urine analysis we found a significant reduction in period 2 compared to period 1, 8 (23%) vs 3 (7%) respectively. Applying a selective and watchful waiting strategy towards urinary catheterization in acute ischemic stroke patients treated with intravenous thrombolysis did not increase the risk of urogenital complications, and the discomfort associated with catheterization was limited to fewer patients.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Eiskjær Sørensen, Kristina","Blauenfeldt, Rolf Ankerlund","Kjøbsted Markvardsen, Anne","Thorsted, Louise Mose","Lehd, Bitten Nørret","Andersen, Grethe"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-137397"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Stroke","Thrombolysis","Urinary Catheterization"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"urinary catheterisation in acute stroke patients treated with intravenous thrombolysis","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-137397","ids":["au-0e0f509e-73d5-4dce-aa02-b0d1d4123b14"],"keyword":["Stroke","Urinary Catheterization","thrombolysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-D8240CE685A65BA9B2EBE5E3228D85B5"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Neurology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristina","key":"eiskjærsørensenpau","last":"Eiskjær Sørensen","locid":"c707b014-a03a-421a-85f1-c9060987aa8b","name":"Eiskjær Sørensen, Kristina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rolf","key":"blauenfeldtpau","last":"Blauenfeldt","locid":"2cc4cfa1-de55-413c-b008-6b5d1c275888","name":"Blauenfeldt, Rolf Ankerlund","name_org":"Blauenfeldt, Rolf","orcid":"0000-0002-4846-9516","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne","key":"kjøbstedmarkvardsenpau","last":"Kjøbsted Markvardsen","locid":"bea441a6-1d4f-4847-9464-80e9a44af2b4","name":"Kjøbsted Markvardsen, Anne","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise Mose","key":"thorstedpau","last":"Thorsted","locid":"2ab70822-56b8-4b78-82e0-5d2ed1584a9e","name":"Thorsted, Louise Mose","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bitten Nørret","key":"lehdpau","last":"Lehd","locid":"1003aca1-3d39-4cdf-b6c0-b6ac280f20b0","name":"Lehd, Bitten Nørret","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Grethe","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"97e157c9-4cb4-4690-a971-06e4699b50fe","name":"Andersen, Grethe","orcid":"0000-0001-6807-2500","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.18261/issn.1892-2686-2020-02-02","issn":["18922678","18922686"],"issn_org":["18922678"],"issue":"2","journal":"Nordisk Sygeplejeforskning","pages":"90-98","publisher":"Universitetsforlaget AS","status":"p","title":"Nordisk Sygeplejeforskning","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-0e0f509e-73d5-4dce-aa02-b0d1d4123b14"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Urinary catheterisation in acute stroke patients treated with intravenous thrombolysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-137397","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Cancer genomics has revealed many genes and core molecular processes that contribute to human malignancies, but the genetic and molecular bases of many rare cancers remains unclear. Genetic predisposition accounts for 5 to 10% of cancer diagnoses in children1,2, and genetic events that cooperate with known somatic driver events are poorly understood. Pathogenic germline variants in established cancer predisposition genes have been recently identified in 5% of patients with the malignant brain tumour medulloblastoma3. Here, by analysing all protein-coding genes, we identify and replicate rare germline loss-of-function variants across ELP1 in 14% of paediatric patients with the medulloblastoma subgroup Sonic Hedgehog (MBSHH).ELP1 was the most common medulloblastoma predisposition gene and increased the prevalence of genetic predisposition to 40% among paediatric patients with MBSHH. Parent–offspring and pedigree analyses identified two families with a history of paediatric medulloblastoma. ELP1-associated medulloblastomas were restricted to the molecular SHHα subtype4 and characterized by universal biallelic inactivation of ELP1 owing to somatic loss of chromosome arm 9q. Most ELP1-associated medulloblastomas also exhibited somatic alterations in PTCH1, which suggests that germline ELP1 loss-of-function variants predispose individuals to tumour development in combination with constitutive activation of SHH signalling. ELP1 is the largest subunit of the evolutionarily conserved Elongator complex, which catalyses translational elongation through tRNA modifications at the wobble (U34) position5,6. Tumours from patients with ELP1-associated MBSHH were characterized by a destabilized Elongator complex, loss of Elongator-dependent tRNA modifications, codon-dependent translational reprogramming, and induction of the unfolded protein response, consistent with loss of protein homeostasis due to Elongator deficiency in model systems7–9. Thus, genetic predisposition to proteome instability may be a determinant in the pathogenesis of paediatric brain cancers. These results support investigation of the role of protein homeostasis in other cancer types and potential for therapeutic interference.","author_count":63,"contributors":["Waszak, Sebastian M.","Robinson, Giles W.","Gudenas, Brian L.","Smith, Kyle S.","Forget, Antoine","Kojic, Marija","Garcia-Lopez, Jesus","Hadley, Jennifer","Hamilton, Kayla V.","Indersie, Emilie","Buchhalter, Ivo","Kerssemakers, Jules","Jäger, Natalie","Sharma, Tanvi","Rausch, Tobias","Kool, Marcel","Sturm, Dominik","Jones, David T.W.","Vasilyeva, Aksana","Tatevossian, Ruth G.","Neale, Geoffrey","Lombard, Bérangère","Loew, Damarys","Nakitandwe, Joy","Rusch, Michael","Bowers, Daniel C.","Bendel, Anne","Partap, Sonia","Chintagumpala, Murali","Crawford, John","Gottardo, Nicholas G.","Smith, Amy","Dufour, Christelle","Rutkowski, Stefan","Eggen, Tone","Wesenberg, Finn","Kjaerheim, Kristina","Feychting, Maria","Lannering, Birgitta","Schüz, Joachim","Johansen, Christoffer","Andersen, Tina V.","Röösli, Martin","Kuehni, Claudia E.","Grotzer, Michael","Remke, Marc","Puget, Stéphanie","Pajtler, Kristian W.","Milde, Till","Witt, Olaf","Ryzhova, Marina","Korshunov, Andrey","Orr, Brent A.","Ellison, David W.","Brugieres, Laurence","Lichter, Peter","Nichols, Kim E.","Gajjar, Amar","Wainwright, Brandon J.","Ayrault, Olivier","Korbel, Jan O.","Northcott, Paul A.","Pfister, Stefan M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-456582"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","France","Germany","Netherlands","Norway","Russia","Sweden","Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Baylor College of Medicine","Cancer Registry of Norway","Children’s Hospitals and Clinics of Minnesota","European Molecular Biology Laboratory","German Cancer Research Cent