{"abstract":"Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome (SSNS) is the most common form of nephrotic syndrome in childhood. Cases with the familial occurrence of SSNS suggest that genetics may play a role in the disease. Human leucocyte antigen (HLA) alleles have been associated with SSNS. We present genetic findings in nine families (44 participants), each with at least two affected siblings. A total of 19 patients were affected with familial SSNS. Six of nine families showed linkage to markers on chromosome 6p (27.29–33.97 Mbp) (Hg19), especially to markers D6S1629 and D6S1560 on HLA dense region in this location. Interestingly, we also found linkage of disease phenotype of familial SSNS on chromosome 15 (91.7–96.9 Mbp) (Hg19) with a logarithm of odds (LOD) score Z = 3.02. Conclusion: Our findings confirm the linkage of HLA markers on chromosome 6, which strengthens the association of HLA alleles in SSNS.What is Known:• Human leukocyte antigen (HLA) alleles have been associated with idiopathic steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome (SSNS). Only few studies have investigated the association between HLA alleles and familial SSNS.What is New:• We present evidence of linkage of familial SSNS to chromosome 6p (27.29–33.97 Mbp) (Hg19), especially to markers D6S1629 and D6S1560 on HLA dense region in this location. We also found linkage of the disease phenotype of familial SSNS on chromosome 15 (91.7–96.9 Mbp) (Hg19) with a logarithm of odds (LOD) score of Z = 3.02 following autosomal recessive inheritance pattern.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Korsgaard, Trine","Joshi, Shivani","Andersen, René Frydensbjerg","Moeller, Kristina","Seeman, Tomás","Podracká, Ludmila","Eiberg, Hans","Rittig, Søren"],"cuid":["S02-099.93-029839"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Korsgaard, Trine"],"country":["Czechia","Germany","Slovakia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charles University","Comenius University","Klinikum Link der Weser"],"keyword":["Human leukocyte antigen"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"human leukocyte antigen association with familial steroid sensitive nephrotic syndrome","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85082074693","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-099.93-029839","identifier":[{"id":"85082074693","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000520900800001","type":"wos"},{"id":"32198629","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-ecc957eb-4eed-444c-9118-7f0c3bd8755b","ku-b4b86df8-2f42-4f3f-a7ad-c179683235c8"],"keyword":["Chromosome 15","Chromosome 6","Familial steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome","Human leukocyte antigen","Whole-genome linkage analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-86225DD556247D4EF6F89DE347B809DA"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"klinikum link der weser","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Klinikum Link der Weser","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cz","key":"charles university","loc":["Czechia","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charles University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"sk","key":"comenius university","loc":["Slovakia","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Comenius University","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Medical Genetics Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Trine","key":"korsgaardpau","last":"Korsgaard","locid":"56247f83-ee6b-4c3d-a57e-c86befa9ee49","name":"Korsgaard, Trine","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Shivani","key":"joshipau","last":"Joshi","locid":"cabef95c-d1f2-42de-9d40-7817a4a40301","name":"Joshi, Shivani","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rene F.","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"5776498e-d2ed-4da7-9030-b111f965e806","name":"Andersen, René Frydensbjerg","name_org":"Andersen, Rene F.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristina","key":"moellerpau","last":"Moeller","name":"Moeller, Kristina","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Tomás","key":"seemanpau","last":"Seeman","name":"Seeman, Tomás","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Ludmila","key":"podrackápau","last":"Podracká","name":"Podracká, Ludmila","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 6","first":"Hans","key":"eibergpau","last":"Eiberg","locid":"40d34cb1-b2ed-4955-a6d2-9b1dff7ca707","name":"Eiberg, Hans","orcid":"0000-0002-4140-1578","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren","key":"rittigpau","last":"Rittig","locid":"04c8afe2-7baf-4f75-a08b-d2ee25d66d09","name":"Rittig, Søren","orcid":"0000-0001-7038-1652","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32198629"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00431-020-03634-3","issn":["03406199","09439676"],"issn_org":["03406199"],"issue":"9","journal":"European Journal of Pediatrics","pages":"1481-1486","publisher":"Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH","status":"p","title":"European Journal of Pediatrics","volume":"179"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85082074693"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ecc957eb-4eed-444c-9118-7f0c3bd8755b","ku-b4b86df8-2f42-4f3f-a7ad-c179683235c8"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Human leukocyte antigen association with familial steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.93-029839","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Here we review contaminant exposure and related health effects in six selected Baltic key species. Sentinel species included are common eider, white-tailed eagle, harbour porpoise, harbour seal, ringed seal and grey seal. The review represents the first attempt of summarizing available information and baseline data for these biomonitoring key species exposed to industrial hazardous substances focusing on anthropogenic persistent organic pollutants (POPs). There was only limited information available for white-tailed eagles and common eider while extensive information exist on POP exposure and health effects in the four marine mammal species. Here we report organ-tissue endpoints (pathologies) and multiple biomarkers used to evaluate health and exposure of key species to POPs, respectively, over the past several decades during which episodes of significant population declines have been reported. Our review shows that POP exposure affects the reproductive system and survival through immune suppression and endocrine disruption, which have led to population-level effects on seals and white-tailed eagles in the Baltic. It is notable that many legacy contaminants, which have been banned for decades, still appear to affect Baltic wildlife. With respect to common eiders, changes in food composition, quality and contaminant exposure seem to have population effects which need to be investigated further, especially during the incubation period where the birds fast. Since new industrial contaminants continuously leak into the environment, we recommend continued monitoring of them in sentinel species in the Baltic, identifying possible effects linked to climate change, and modelling of population level effects of contaminants and climate change.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Sonne, Christian","Siebert, Ursula","Gonnsen, Katharina","Desforges, Jean-Pierre","Eulaers, Igor","Persson, Sara","Roos, Anna","Bäcklin, Britt Marie","Kauhala, Kaarina","Tange Olsen, Morten","Harding, Karin C.","Treu, Gabriele","Galatius, Anders","Andersen-Ranberg, Emilie","Gross, Stephanie","Lakemeyer, Jan","Lehnert, Kristina","Lam, Su Shiung","Peng, Wanxi","Dietz, Rune"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-013554"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Sonne, Christian"],"country":["China","Finland","Germany","Malaysia","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Federal Environmental Agency, Germany","German Environment Agency","Henan Agricultural University","Luke Natural Resources Institute Finland","Natural Resources Institute Finland (Luke)","Swedish Museum of Natural History","Universiti Malaysia Terengganu","University of Gothenburg","University of Veterinary Medicine Hannover, Foundation"],"keyword":["Baltic Sea","Biological effects","Contaminants","Exposure","OCP","Organochlorine pesticides","PCBs","POPs","Persistent organic pollutants","Polychlorinated biphenyls"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"health effects from contaminant exposure in baltic sea birds and marine mammals a review","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2020.105725","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-013554","identifier":[{"id":"32311628","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85083297123","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0d309aa2-8c7f-4d82-b32c-5371fccee5cd","ku-77869d21-4922-4685-9226-d4df90f0f638"],"keyword":["Baltic Sea","Biological effects","Contaminants","Exposure","OCP","Organochlorine pesticides","PCBs","POPs","Persistent org