ark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a091bec8-a401-40f8-95a3-48ac74c34845","ku-bf7a4619-edd8-43c6-b194-436b659a46ea"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Comparison of cultured human cardiomyocyte clusters obtained from embryos/fetuses or derived from human embryonic stem cells","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.02-024421","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Importance: Long-term health effects of cancer in adult survivors are a major concern; however, it is difficult to differentiate between the consequences of cancer and cancer treatment and those of normal aging or comorbidity. Objectives: To provide an overview and investigate the temporal pattern of hospitalizations for medically verified incident somatic disease in adult survivors compared with cancer-free comparison people, taking into consideration pretreatment comorbidity and the socioeconomic position of the participants. Design, Setting, and Participants: In this nationwide, population-based cohort-cohort study, 458646 survivors of the 12 most frequent first primary cancers listed in the Danish Cancer Registry between January 1, 1997, and December 31, 2014, and 2121567 matched cancer-free comparison people were identified from the Danish Central Population Registry. Hospitalizations for somatic diseases after cancer diagnosis or study entry were identified from the National Patient Register and stratified according to the 11 main diagnostic groups in the International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems, Tenth Revision. A cohort of cancer survivors was formed for each combination of cancer type and diagnostic group, with a corresponding group of cancer-free people, resulting in 132 unique cohorts. Data analysis was performed from September 1, 2017, to January 15, 2018. Main Outcomes and Measures: Risk of hospitalization and the temporal pattern of incidence were analyzed in Cox proportional hazards regression models. Cumulative incidence proportions were calculated by the pseudo-observation method. Results: A total of 2580213 people were investigated, of whom 458646 were cancer survivors (mean [SD] age, 69 [11.6] years; 230 793 [50.3%] male and 227 853 [49.7%] female) and 2 121 567 were comparison people (mean [SD] age, 69 [11.5] years; 1 054 465 [49.7%] male and 1 067 102 [50.3%] female). More cancer survivors vs comparison people had comorbid conditions at the time of cancer diagnosis or study entry (Charlson Comorbidity Index ≥1: 19% vs 13%). Overall, the risk of hospitalizations for somatic diseases was significantly higher for cancer survivors in almost all diagnostic groups (eg, diseases in the nervous system among breast cancer survivors: hazard ratio, 1.20; 95% CI, 1.17-1.22; diseases in the respiratory system in lung cancer survivors: hazard ratio, 5.85; 95% CI, 5.63-6.07; and diseases in blood and blood-forming organs in prostate cancer survivors: hazard ratio, 2.60; 95% CI, 2.50-2.71). Conclusions and Relevance: The findings suggest that adult survivors of the 12 most common cancers are at significantly higher risk for a broad range of somatic diseases that require hospitalization compared with matched cancer-free comparison people. The results of this study suggest the importance of close, targeted monitoring for new somatic disease during follow-up care of cancer survivors.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Kjaer, Trille Kristina","Andersen, Elisabeth Anne Wreford","Winther, Jeanette Falck","Bidstrup, Pernille Envold","Borre, Michael","Møller, Henrik","Larsen, Signe Benzon","Johansen, Christoffer","Dalton, Susanne Oksbjerg"],"cuid":["S02-099.68-015917"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Kjaer, Trille Kristina"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term somatic disease risk in adult danish cancer survivors","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"http://europepmc.org/Copyright","type":"rem","url":"http://europepmc.org/article/PMC/6459216?pdf=render","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.68-015917","identifier":[{"id":"30844031","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85062661307","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6459216","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-2d5464b3-d40a-4b9d-8bf2-0b4e85d2f3af","ku-6b7cd1b1-beb4-4e7d-95e0-8f77e841bfa0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-BE41E452CC23BF89EDF18BF94C163AC2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"http://europepmc.org/Copyright","type":"rem","url":"http://europepmc.org/article/PMC/6459216?pdf=render","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6459216/?report=printable","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-CAN","l3nn":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","namevar":["Danish Cancer Society"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-CAN","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Biostatistics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Trille Kristina","key":"kjaerpau","last":"Kjaer","name":"Kjaer, Trille Kristina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Elisabeth Anne Wreford","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"456c37e7-1e18-49dc-adb2-0d90a5982f30","name":"Andersen, Elisabeth Anne Wreford","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette Falck","key":"wintherpau","last":"Winther","locid":"11ca8ef7-98c5-4eed-9dbe-3b44124f948b","name":"Winther, Jeanette Falck","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Pernille Envold","key":"bidstruppau","last":"Bidstrup","locid":"7cef3894-8d41-4b9b-acec-95b1d1b3f890","name":"Bidstrup, Pernille Envold","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Michael","key":"borrepau","last":"Borre","locid":"f0a95cf0-1ff2-4308-991f-749123c14ba5","name":"Borre, Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","name":"Møller, Henrik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Signe Benzon","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Signe Benzon","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Christoffer","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","locid":"18d62c73-2f35-499d-ac94-134ecd806296","name":"Johansen, Christoffer","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Susanne Oksbjerg","key":"daltonpau","last":"Dalton","locid":"84d10468-fe5d-4cc7-952f-1d89d961390f","name":"Dalton, Susanne Oksbjerg","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30844031"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1001/jamaoncol.2018.7192","issn":["23742437","23742445"],"issn_org":["23742437"],"issue":"4","journal":"JAMA Oncology","pages":"537-545","publisher":"American Medical Association","status":"p","title":"JAMA Oncology","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85062661307"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Non-Profit Organisations","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-2d5464b3-d40a-4b9d-8bf2-0b4e85d2f3af","ku-6b7cd1b1-beb4-4e7d-95e0-8f77e841bfa0"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Long-term Somatic Disease Risk in Adult Danish Cancer Survivors","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.68-015917","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Document recommendation systems for locating relevant literature have mostly relied on methods developed a decade ago. This is largely due to the lack of a large offline gold-standard benchmark of relevant documents that cover a variety of research fields such that newly developed literature search techniques can be compared, improved and translated into practice. To overcome this bottleneck, we have established the RElevant LIterature SearcH consortium consisting of more than 1500 scientists from 84 countries, who have collectively annotated the relevance of over 180 000 PubMed-listed articles with regard to their respective seed (input) article/s. The majority of annotations were contributed by highly experienced, original authors of the seed articles. The collected data cover 76\\% of all unique PubMed Medical Subject Headings descriptors. No systematic biases were observed across different experience levels, research fields or time spent on annotations. More importantly, annotations of the same document pairs contributed by different scientists were highly concordant. We further show that the three representative baseline methods used to generate recommended articles for evaluation (Okapi Best Matching 25, Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency and PubMed Related Articles) had similar overall performances. Additionally, we found that these methods each tend to produce distinct collections of recommended articles, suggesting that a hybrid method may be required to completely capture all relevant articles. The established database server located at https://relishdb.ict.griffith.edu.au is freely available for the downloading of annotation data and the blind testing of new methods. We expect that this benchmark will be useful for stimulating the development of new powerful techniques for title and title/abstract-based search engines for relevant articles in biomedical research.","author_count":1580,"contributors":["Brown, Peter","Tan, Aik-Choon","El-